//
// Normative rule pm_mxr_exception (src/priv/zpm.adoc):
//   When MXR is in effect at the effective privilege mode where explicit memory access is
//     performed, pointer masking does not apply.
//
// Context:
//   S-mode accesses. With MXR=1 pointer masking does not apply to S/U accesses, even with Bare
//     translation (MXR has no permission effect there). The rule names every explicit access, so
//     stores and AMOs are unmasked too. MXR is read-only zero without S-mode; the test reads it
//     back and expects masking when it did not stick. PMM is WARL: after each write the test reads
//     the value the hart holds (00, 10 or 11; 01 is reserved) and checks the behavior of that
//     value, so harts that support only some PMLEN values are tested on those.
//
// Clarifications:
//   https://github.com/riscv/riscv-isa-manual/issues/3313 and https://github.com/riscv/riscv-isa-
//     manual/issues/1915: MXR is never in effect for an access whose effective privilege mode is M,
//     so mstatus.MXR does not disable M-mode pointer masking (aswaterman: otherwise "S-mode could
//     disable M-mode pointer masking by setting sstatus.MXR"; gfavor: "mstatus.MXR is never in
//     effect for M-mode"). For an S/U effective mode, MXR=1 disables pointer masking even with
//     satp.MODE=Bare; for VS/VU, sstatus.MXR or vsstatus.MXR.
//
// Test plan:
//   1. MXR=1. For PMM written 11 and 10: ld, sd and amoadd.d (Zaamo) of page A with the top 7 bits
//     all ones and with bits 63:48 = 0xA5C3: not masked: access faults.
//   2. MXR=0, PMM written 11: ld and sd are masked again (transition back).
//   3. Sv39 (when supported): the same with MXR=1 through the identity map and the high-half alias.
//
// Approximate dynamic instruction count: 11031
//

